    [2] reflection targets — RULED OUT: org.scilab.modules.core.Scilab and
        org.scilab.modules.scinotes.SciNotes are each reached only via
        Class.forName/loadClass STRING literals (confirmed by reading the
        call sites), never imported. core is Wave D and scinotes is Wave E —
        neither is migrated yet, and a compile-time dependency on either
        here would be premature (core) or would likely cycle (scinotes,
        which itself depends on gui). Reproduced as an absence, matching
        every earlier module's Class.forName pattern for an unmigrated
        module (see e.g. modules/console/pom.xml's HelpSearchField entry for
        the same shape one wave earlier). java.awt.Color and
        org.scilab.modules.gui.ged.graphic_objects. are the tool's own
        reflection regex matching an unrelated Class.forName/loadClass call
        and a trailing-dot package prefix respectively — neither names a
        Scilab module, both irrelevant to <dependencies>.
    [3] fully-qualified uses with no import — mostly the ten reactor
        packages above spelled out in full elsewhere in the tree (nothing
        new), plus the vendored-jar classes catalogued below. One entry is a
        confirmed NON-finding: every org.scilab.modules.ihm.* name (UIElement,
        utils.Position, utils.Size) in this list resolves entirely to
        `@see` javadoc tags inside ScilabBridge.java (grep-confirmed: every
        hit sits on a `* @see org.scilab.modules.ihm...` line) — no such
        module exists anywhere in this tree, and zero compile-time weight
        attaches to a javadoc @see target. This is the same class of
        over-report the tool's own header warns about (graphic_objects/gui,
        Stage 2-b's core.Scilab), just caught here before it could be
        mistaken for a dependency.
  No OTHER manifest.class-path anywhere else in this tree changed shape —
  gui's own value is exactly the six entries handled below.

  THE cc.sosonline.gpu FINDING — a first-party jar, not a third-party one.
  Two files (bridge/canvas/SwingScilabVulkanCanvas.java, bridge/canvas/
  ScilabCanvasFactory.java) import exactly three classes — GpuSurfaceComponent,
  NativeSurface, VulkanScene — from package cc.sosonline.gpu, which exists
  NOWHERE as source in this tree (confirmed: no `package cc.sosonline.gpu`
  anywhere under modules/). It is supplied by thirdparty/
  swing-gpu-surface-0.1.0.jar, itself Maven-built (its own manifest reads
  "Created-By: Maven JAR Plugin 3.5.0") and — per fetch-thirdparty.sh's own
  comment — "the first-party Layer-1 GPU surface jar is *tracked* in this
  repo (vendored)" at firstparty/swing-gpu-surface-0.1.0.jar (moved there from
  modules/prebuildjava/ when Ant was retired), i.e. part of THIS project's own Vulkan/MoltenVK
  renderer work (docs/design/vulkan-renderer.md), not an external library.
  Every method gui's two consumers actually call against GpuSurfaceComponent/
  NativeSurface/VulkanScene (constructors, .width()/.height()/.handle(),
  .resize()/.beginFrame()/.triangles()/.lines()/.depthEpochs()/
  .uploadTexture()/.destroyTexture()/.sprite()/.image()/.endFrame()/
  .dispose()/.snapshot()/.captureNext()/.ensureContext()) is typed in
  primitives, arrays, java.nio.ByteBuffer or java.awt/BufferedImage — never
  an LWJGL or Kotlin type — so, unlike the GLBuffers case below,
  swing-gpu-surface-0.1.0.jar alone satisfies this compile with no need for
  the lwjgl/lwjgl-vulkan/kotlin-stdlib/jna/slf4j-api/annotations/
  directory-watcher jars that build.incl.xml's shared compile.classpath also
  carries (those are swing-gpu-surface's OWN build-time dependencies, not
  gui's — confirmed by the full-reactor build below actually succeeding
  without them).

  THE JOGL / GLUEGEN PAIR — gui imports com.jogamp.opengl{,.awt} directly
  (GL, GLAutoDrawable, GLCapabilities, GLException, GLProfile, GLCanvas,
  GLJPanel — the classic JOGL canvas, alongside the Vulkan one above) and
